 is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413266 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413266 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71037" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413266 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413266 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413266 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2f4d4cbd-f2d7-5e35-a92b-2f2fb49fe824" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413266 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-0}{4}=\\frac{y-(-3)}{0}=\\frac{z-4}{1}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y+3}{0}=z-4,\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{y+3}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=-3:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4+t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{y+3}{0}$ part and instead write $y=-3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}=z-4,\\,y=-3\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413267 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413267 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127464" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413267 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413267 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the straight line that passes through $P(7,-1,0)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨-3,1,-4⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413267 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "999b5494-410f-5564-a5e2-d9dbe0ae34c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413267 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{-3}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{1}=\\frac{z-0}{-4}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{-3}=\\frac{y+1}{1}=\\frac{z}{-4}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413268 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413268 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127076" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413268 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413268 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the parametric equations of the line $\\frac{x-10}{-6}=\\frac{y+5}{3}=\\frac{z+7}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413268 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bc81db7e-a314-5809-b5c5-608d6bc1d824" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413268 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-10}{-6}=\\frac{y-(-5)}{3}=\\frac{z-(-7)}{2},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(10,-5,-7)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨-6,3,2⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations are the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=10-6t \\\\ y=-5+3t \\\\ z=-7+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413269 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413269 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127074" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413269 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413269 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the parametric equations of the line $\\frac{x}{7}=\\frac{y-3}{2}=\\frac{z+8}{-6}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413269 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ca426087-2ced-544a-8fc8-80a74f2884ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413269 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{7}=\\frac{y-3}{2}=\\frac{z-(-8)}{-6},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(0,3,-8)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨7,2,-6⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations are the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=7t \\\\ y=3+2t \\\\ z=-8-6t\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413270 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413270 #edb/ref 1069 "q-33246" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413270 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413270 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the straight line with the parametric equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3-4t \\\\ y=5+2t \\\\ z=-3+6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413270 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cf27c214-ec1e-55e8-948b-06e561f6f7e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413270 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3-4t \\\\ 5+2t \\\\ -3+6t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-4t \\\\ 2t \\\\ 6t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(3,5,-3)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨-4,2,6⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-4}=\\frac{y-5}{2}=\\frac{z-(-3)}{6}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-4}=\\frac{y-5}{2}=\\frac{z+3}{6}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413271 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413271 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127459" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413271 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413271 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413271 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dbddc051-609a-50f8-b7fd-7c6e4c6d692b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413271 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{0}=\\frac{y-(-2)}{1}=\\frac{z-0}{-5}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{0}=\\frac{y+2}{1}=\\frac{z}{-5},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{x-7}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $x$ component always maintains the constant value of $x=7:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2+t \\\\ -5t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{x-7}{0}$ part and instead write $x=7,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{y+2}{1}=\\frac{z}{-5},\\,x=7\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413272 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413272 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127460" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413272 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413272 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413272 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e1761a52-f68d-5dfd-83a7-02fd5760a09e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413272 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y-(-6)}{5}=\\frac{z-13}{0}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y+6}{5}=\\frac{z-13}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{z-13}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=13:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}1-2t \\\\ -6+5t \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{z-13}{0}$ part and instead write $z=13$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y+6}{5},\\,z=13\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413273 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413273 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127073" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413273 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413273 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the parametric equations of the line $\\frac{x-13}{-7}=\\frac{y+2}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{5}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413273 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e2f7b0c6-34b9-5cdd-a2c3-dccbfbacb488" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413273 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-13}{-7}=\\frac{y-(-2)}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{5},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(13,-2,1)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨-7,3,5⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations are the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=13-7t \\\\ y=-2+3t \\\\ z=1+5t\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
